99 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (Road infrastructure is crucial for transportation, but potholes pose a significant threat to both vehicles and pedestrians. Traditional methods of pothole detection and reporting are often manual and time-consuming. The proposed system utilizes IoT technology to automate this process, ensuring swift and accurate detection of potholes.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 64 0 R
/E (    Road infrastructure is a critical component of modern society, facilitating transportation and economic activity. However, the presence of potholes significantly compromises road quality, leading to various detrimental effects such as increased accident rates, vehicle damage, and traffic disruptions. Traditional methods of pothole detection often involve manual inspection, which is time-consuming, labor- intensive, and prone to human error. With the advent of the Internet of Things \(IoT\), there is a significant opportunity to develop automated, efficient, and real-time solutions for infrastructure monitoring. This project focuses on designing and implementing an IoT-based system for the detection of potholes and the subsequent generation of alerts to relevant stakeholders.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 65 0 R
/T (2. Background                 Previous research and existing solutions for road anomaly detection often rely on image processing, vibration analysis, or a combination thereof. Image-based systems typically involve mounting cameras on vehicles, capturing road images, and using computer vision algorithms to identify potholes. While effective, these systems can be computationally intensive and sensitive to lighting conditions. Vibration- based systems, on the other hand, use accelerometers to measure vehicle vibrations, correlating unusual patterns with road defects. The integration of GPS allows for the precise localization of detected anomalies. Our system builds upon these principles by leveraging the ubiquity of IoT devices to provide a scalable, real-time, and location-aware solution.)
/E (2. Background                 Previous research and existing solutions for road anomaly detection often rely on image processing, vibration analysis, or a combination thereof. Image-based systems typically involve mounting cameras on vehicles, capturing road images, and using computer vision algorithms to identify potholes. While effective, these systems can be computationally intensive and sensitive to lighting conditions. Vibration- based systems, on the other hand, use accelerometers to measure vehicle vibrations, correlating unusual patterns with road defects. The integration of GPS allows for the precise localization of detected anomalies. Our system builds upon these principles by leveraging the ubiquity of IoT devices to provide a scalable, real-time, and location-aware solution.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
